{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r11s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20260930-002-r11s1\ntitle: \"Bank the next-PC table into shallow asynchronous memories\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 191.13 iter/sec at median 77.593 MHz and\n  4,059,766 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Recent attempts to delay predictor\n  maintenance or separate EX producer/control lanes regressed. A temporary\n  early-PC retiming prototype also regressed to median 71.162 MHz.\n  Its vendor timing report exposes the structural issue: a path starts\n  at ID/EX rs2_val, traverses branch comparison and resolved_next_pc,\n  and ends at the predictor target memory's BSRAM ADB read-address pin.\n  The inferred large table folds a PC boundary into synchronous memory\n  addressing. A flat flop-table prototype removed that path but lost\n  timing through high-fanout parallel tag lookup. Shallow memory banking\n  offers a way to retain compact storage and a real asynchronous lookup.\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure only the storage topology of the existing 64-entry fully\n  tagged next-PC predictor into four independent 16-entry banks backed\n  by asynchronous distributed memory. Preserve the exact predictor\n  algorithm, table capacity, full tags/targets, fetch schedule and\n  immediate EX recovery/training. This is one structural memory-banking\n  hypothesis, with no extra instruction stage or prediction-policy change.\n\n  Split pc[7:2] into bank pc[7:6] and row pc[5:2]; split train_pc the\n  same way. Each bank holds sixteen 24-bit tags, sixteen 30-bit aligned\n  targets, sixteen conditional flags and sixteen two-bit saturating\n  counters, plus a sixteen-bit resettable validity vector. Read all\n  banks at the lookup row in parallel, and use a shallow four-way bank\n  selection. Keep full pc[31:8] tag checks and the original unconditional\n  or counter-MSB taken rule. The chosen bank supplies its stored target\n  when predicted taken and PC+4 otherwise. Use Gowin's supported\n  distributed-memory inference attribute on the shallow payload arrays;\n  retain portable behavioral RTL for Verilator and riscv-formal.\n\n  Tr\n…[3418 of 6490 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c18f5c4a697f0ae6]…\nl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the 64-row indexed predictor payload arrays with four\n      16-row asynchronous distributed-memory banks, local training\n      reads/updates, parallel bank lookups and shallow bank selection.\n      Preserve full-width policy, old-data semantics, event timing,\n      fetch queue, saved predictions and immediate recovery priority.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing queue and saved-prediction checks; add independent\n      original-table model coverage across all bank/row combinations,\n      tag aliases, counter transitions, interbank update collisions,\n      invalidation and simultaneous lookup/training.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every ISA/RVFI program and expectation. Extend bank alias\n      and training-event coverage under queue, load/M and bus holds,\n      checking exact prediction recovery, source/result/next-PC values,\n      contiguous retirement and exactly-once memory side effects.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
