{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r1s0 (regression, +3.38%): Halving the combinational divider's digit count left a serial borrow-driven subtraction/priority-mux chain on Gowin's critical path, so shared DIV/REM reduced LUT4s by 45.7% but gained only 3.4% Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 (improvement, +609.08%): Replacing combinational `/` and `%` with a registered eight-iteration divider capped at four restoring steps per clock shortened the EX critical path enough for the Fmax gain to outweigh CoreMark's extra divide cycles.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r1s2 (regression, +5.69%): The 32-entry tagged next-PC predictor cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.3%, but the forwarding/combinational DIV/REM path still limited Fmax to 5.393 MHz, so removing control-flow bubbles could not match the registered-divider design.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r2s1 (improvement, +20.29%): Two radix-4 digits per iteration with parallel D/2D/3D subtraction and a response register before sign correction shorten the divider-to-EX/MEM path, while a four-bit launch seed preserves nine-clock EX latency.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r3s1 (regression, +7.87%): Squashing only ID/EX valid while capturing raw imem_data independently of flush removes late branch-to-decode/operand feedback and raised prototype Fmax 7.87% at unchanged CoreMark cycles; load-use holds must use imem_ready rather than redirect-masked fetch validity to keep redirect out of payload enables.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (improvement, +13.90%): Pairing 64-entry tagged next-PC prediction with the retimed divider and validity-only ID/EX recovery converts CoreMark's taken-transfer bubble savings into a throughput gain by keeping redirect out of instruction decode and payload capture enables.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (improvement, +6.38%): Registering forwarded operands and one shared unsigned 32x32 product before carry-save signed-high correction removes multiplication from the fast ALU mux, letting the clock gain outweigh C\n…[1789 of 4861 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:dcc1777d481ae9de]…\nd saved responses.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r11s0 (improvement, +5.11%): Balanced trees of two-bit LUT4 comparison leaves with signed_lt = unsigned_lt ^ (a[31] ^ b[31]) shorten branch and SLT/SLTU paths on Gowin by sharing signed/unsigned predicates within each consumer, improving Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20260930-002-r12s1 (regression, -6.40%): Fifteen parallel 37-bit subtractors and a balanced 16-way remainder OR tree cut divider path cells from 67 to 39 but increased its delay and exposed EX-to-dmem timing, showing that reduced serial depth alone does not guarantee higher Gowin Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20260930-003-r12s2 (regression, -3.76%): Parallel carry-consistency checks for sequential/direct/JALR prediction matches added 141 LUT4s while the unchanged divider remained critical, showing that removing verification carry chains cannot improve whole-core timing when another cone limits Fmax (78.489 versus 81.557 MHz at 4,059,766 CoreMark cycles).\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
