{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_38", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r4s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20260930-001-r4s0\ntitle: \"Balanced DSP-sized partial products for single-cycle RV32M multiply\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 117.75 at 49.426 MHz and 4,197,517\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported 27.030 ns worst path starts\n  at the register file and crosses ID, EX, the ALU, and EX before a\n  memory endpoint. The registered divider and validity-only recovery\n  have removed the earlier deeply serial divider and squash cones.\n  The remaining ALU still describes three independently signed full\n  products, and all recent implementations use six DSP blocks. A\n  multiplier built from concurrent native-size partial products and\n  an explicit compressor tree is a promising local way to shorten\n  arithmetic and reduce DSP routing without adding execution cycles.\n  The log's previous micro_opt attempts addressed division or squash;\n  none tested this multiplier organization. The module-level timing\n  summary implicates the ALU but does not prove multiplication is the\n  critical operation; vendor cell-level timing must check that premise.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the real MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU combinational multiplier\n  cone with one shared, balanced partial-product implementation. Keep\n  the champion predictor, divider, pipeline boundaries, single-cycle\n  multiply latency, forwarding, stalls, and nret=1 contract unchanged.\n  This is a micro-optimization of arithmetic mapping, with no new\n  transaction protocol, execution stage, or speculative behavior.\n\n  Decompose each source into its low unsigned 16-bit half and upper\n  16-bit half. Make the upper-half signedness depend on the operation:\n  signed A and B for MULH; signed A and unsigned B for MULHSU; unsigned\n  A and B for MULHU and MUL. Represent each upper half as signed 17\n  bits, using {sign_enable && operand[31], operand[31:16]}; represent\n  each lower half as signed 17 bits with a zero leading bit. Compute\n  four concurrent products LL, LH, HL, and HH, each fitting one native\n  18x18 multiplier. Use explicitly size\n…[3693 of 6765 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9d8ff89e24a60550]…\nrrent DSP-sized half products, explicit\n      sign extension, a two-level carry-save tree, and one final addition\n      over the upper 48 columns. Share the product across all four\n      multiply variants, preserving one-cycle execution and every exact\n      ALTOPS formula. Leave other ALU operations and division unchanged.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all existing tests and extend independent multiplication\n      checks with all sign combinations, half-word boundaries, carry\n      chains, extreme operands, and deterministic random vectors for\n      MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU in real and ALTOPS builds.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add sequences of different multiply variants with forwarded ALU\n      and loaded sources, dependent ALU/store/branch consumers, and\n      imem/dmem backpressure. Verify exact results and RVFI operands,\n      contiguous order, and exactly-once effects without weakening\n      existing latency, trap, memory, CRC, or progress checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
