{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"agent_message","text":"The final prototype measured a median 72.124 MHz across Gowin’s three placements. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical validation checks with the same 4,059,766 bracketed cycles, giving a projected fitness of about 177.65 iter/sec—27.5% above the champion. I’m recording the operand-boundary change as the structural hypothesis; full formal validation remains for implementation."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml, jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r7s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20260930-002-r7s1',\n'title':'Complete the registered operand delivery boundary',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 139.32 iter/sec at 56.56 MHz with 4,059,766\nbracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path starts at the\nEX/MEM destination tag and crosses forwarding, EX and predictor update\nlogic: 24.328 ns through 39 cells. The previous flop register-file\nattempt exposed WB-tag-to-divider launch and gained only 4.14 percent,\nbelow acceptance; delayed predictor training regressed. These results\nshow that fixing one operand-delivery component leaves another serial\nsource selection on the EX path. The promising structural angle is to\nmake the existing ID/EX operand boundary complete for both register\nvalues and dependency control, including a preselected WB bypass value.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Restructure operand delivery around the existing ID/EX boundary,\nwithout adding an instruction stage or changing execution occupancy.\nImplement one integrated registered operand-delivery structure with\nthree necessary parts: explicit architectural flop words so source\nreads finish before ID/EX, dependency matches registered alongside\nID/EX so register-tag equality finishes before EX, and a selected\nwriteback value registered at MEM/WB so the WB data source does not\nrequire another load-versus-ALU mux before EX forwarding. Preserve\nthe existing fetch queue, predictor policy, fast ALU and blocking M\nunits. This is a single structural retiming of the source-delivery\nnetwork; each component removes a serial selection that otherwise\ncontinues across the intended boundary.\n\nIn reg_file.sv use 31 explicit, individually declared 32-bit scalar\nflop words with individual synchronous write-address qualification,\nreset-to-zero and two complete 32-address case reads. Keep x0 literal\nzero and both existing same-cycle WB write-first bypasses. Finish all\nread and bypass selection before the existing ID/EX operand registers;\nintroduce no bank-selection mux after them. Avoid a variable-indexed\nstorage array that Gowin can replace with BSRAM. Keep the public\nreg_file interface and all architectural reset/write/read semantics.\n\nIn forward_unit.sv register four Boolean dependency matches. When\nID/EX captures a new payload (\"'!stall_id), for each queue-head source\nS compare (S != 0) && (S == id_ex_w.rd) for the next EX/MEM producer,\nand (S != 0) && (S == ex_mem_w.rd) for the next MEM/WB producer.\nThese are the destinations that actually move on that edge; a MUL/DIV\nresponse has the held ID/EX destination saved at its request capture.\nUse if_id_w.instr source addresses, including queued heads, rather\nthan raw imem_data. Match registers share decode payload capture,\nnot a redirect-qualified enable. Reset clears all four matches.\n\nWhen stall_id holds ID/EX and stall_ex_mem holds EX/MEM, retain all\nfour matches because EX/MEM and the MEM/WB forwarding payload stay\nstationary. When ID/EX is held while older stages advance, migrate\nits EX/MEM matches to its MEM/WB matches and clear its EX/MEM matches,\nmatching the draining EX/MEM bubble during MUL/DIV waits. A load-use\nhold also clears ID/EX valid, so no consumer executes with stale\nmatches; recompute matches on the dependent instruction'\"'s later\ncapture. Redirect invalidation still wins over a coincident hold.\nNever refresh a held instruction from the younger queue head.\n\nAt EX, preserve the existing three-way forwarding mux interface and\nEX/MEM-over-MEM/WB-over-base priority. Select EX/MEM only when its\nregistered match and actual valid/ctrl.reg_write eligibility agree;\nselect MEM/WB only when its match and retained ctrl.reg_write agree.\nThe nonzero-source match suppresses x0 producers. Keep MEM/WB\nforwarding independent of retirement valid: valid-cleared history is\nrequired while an older memory operation stalls. Actual producer\neligibility, rather than an early raw equality alone, must suppress\nnon-writing and trapped producers. Remove all five-bit tag equality\nfrom the post-ID/EX EX operand selection path. Fully forwarded MUL/DIV\noperands and RVFI source values are still saved exactly once at launch.\n\nAdd a 32-bit wb_data field to mem_wb_t. On a normal MEM/WB capture,\nregister wb_data <= in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result.\nwb_stage drives w_data directly from that field while preserving its\ncurrent w_en and destination logic. Reset initializes the new field;\ndmem hold preserves it with all other forwarding payload while\nclearing only valid. Retain the existing alu_result/read_data and\nRVFI metadata fields as needed; synthesis may prune redundant fields.\nBoth RF writes and WB-to-EX bypass use this selected registered value.\nDo not add an execution clock or change the load-use bubble.\n\nAn isolated prototype implementing exactly these three parts changed\nonly reg_file.sv, forward_unit.sv, core.sv, core_pkg.sv, mem_stage.sv\nand wb_stage.sv. All 39 unchanged pipeline cocotb cases and five\nunchanged register-file cases passed. The unchanged ISS cosim accepted\nall 115 selftest retirements under both bus-stall flags. The unchanged\ncanonical 2K, -O3, ten-iteration CoreMark evaluator accepted UART text,\nall expected CRCs, access bounds and both MMIO timing brackets, with\nexactly 4,059,766 bracketed cycles, identical to the champion.\n\nUsing unchanged core_bench_si.sv, the timed stall generator and vendor\nflow, Gowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 68.145/72.124/72.124 MHz,\na median 72.124 MHz. Option 0 used 4905 LUT4s, 1925 FFs, 24 LUT-RAMs,\nseven BSRAMs and two DSPs. Median Fmax times ten iterations divided by\n4,059,766 cycles projects approximately 177.65 iter/sec, 27.5 percent\nabove 139.32. The worst option-0 path moved to producer eligibility\nand predictor update, with 19.969 ns arrival, instead of the original\nregister-tag or RF-memory operand path. These are proposal-stage\nmeasurements; unchanged formal and full implementation-phase gates\nremain mandatory before acceptance.\n\nPreserve nret=1 and the exact core IO, sole channel-0 retirement,\nstrict order +1, decoder default-illegal discipline, EBREAK-only SYSTEM\nlegality, alignment traps, memory-request stability, one-time side\neffects, validity-only decode recovery, reset cancellation, exact\nnine-edge DIV and three-edge MUL transactions, ALTOPS formulas and\nreal RV32M arithmetic. Keep every existing ten-clock ready-bus division\nretirement check. Do not change arithmetic, predictor learning,\nfetch occupancy or any checker to obtain the timing result.\n\nRetain all existing tests. Add focused registered-match transitions\nfor both sources, younger writers to the same rd, x0 and non-writers,\nqueued decode, WB collisions, load-use bubbles, dmem holds with\nvalid-cleared WB history, MUL/DIV producer drain and response-edge\nconsumers, redirects and reset. Check results and RVFI operands against\nindependent architectural expectations and verify each store and\nretirement once. Run the unchanged formal suite and full cosim gates,\nthen repeat canonical stalled CoreMark and all three Gowin placements.\nAt unchanged cycles acceptance needs median Fmax above 59.162 MHz and\nfitness above 145.729 iter/sec. Forecast a 27 percent fitness gain,\nwith medium confidence pending formal. Remaining predictor eligibility,\ndivider recurrence and added flop routing can limit final timing;\nre-measure cycles and median Fmax rather than assuming the prototype.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':27,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Replace inferred storage with 31 explicit scalar resettable flop\nwords and complete case reads. Keep x0, synchronous writes, both WB\nwrite-first bypasses and the existing interface. Complete all source\nselection before ID/EX, preserving architectural reset semantics.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'''Replace live EX register-tag comparisons with four match registers\ncomputed for accepted queue-head sources against destinations moving\nto EX/MEM and MEM/WB. Freeze on dmem hold; shift EX matches to WB and\nclear EX matches when older stages drain during a decode hold. Keep\nactual write qualification, younger priority and retained WB history.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire clock/reset, stall_id, stall_ex_mem, queue-head source addresses,\ncurrent ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations, and actual producer write\neligibility to the retimed forwarding unit. Keep EX forwarding-select\noutputs and all external IO, pipeline movement and nret=1 retirement.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add a 32-bit preselected wb_data field to mem_wb_t, preserving all\nexisting pipeline and RVFI fields and compilation-unit compatibility.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Register the load-versus-ALU result selection into MEM/WB.wb_data\non normal capture. Reset it and retain it under dmem hold along with\nthe existing payload while clearing only valid. Keep load extraction,\nalignment traps, byte lanes, side effects and RVFI metadata unchanged.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/wb_stage.sv','description':'''Drive w_data directly from MEM/WB.wb_data instead of selecting between\nread_data and alu_result after the register. Preserve current write\nenable and destination behavior; RF and EX bypass use this same value.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Retain all current reset, both-read-port, write-first and x0 tests.\nExtend address-spanning reads/writes and simultaneous WB collisions\nwithout changing expected combinational-read/synchronous-write timing.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_forward_unit.py','description':'''Add an isolated clocked forwarding-control suite for capture, both\nsource matches, younger priority, non-writers, x0, producer migration,\ndmem hold, retained WB eligibility, response-edge capture and reset.\nUse an independent producer/consumer sequence model for selection.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve every current architectural, queue, predictor, arithmetic,\nlatency and RVFI assertion. Extend repeated-writer/both-source hazards,\nqueued sources, WB collisions, valid-cleared forwarding history and\nMUL/DIV drain/response consumers; verify exact operands, side effects,\nretirement order and unchanged ten-clock ready-bus division bounds.\n'''}\n]}\nclass ReadableDumper(yaml.SafeDumper):\n pass\ndef represent_str(dumper,value):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',value,style='|' if '\\\\n' in value else None)\nReadableDumper.add_representer(str,represent_str)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(h,Dumper=ReadableDumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\nparsed=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(parsed,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert parsed['id']=='hyp-20260930-002-r7s1'\nassert parsed['category']=='structural'\nassert type(parsed['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nassert -50 <= parsed['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct'] <= 50\nassert all(c['file'].startswith(('rtl/','test/test_')) for c in parsed['changes'])\nprint('Wrote and schema-validated',p)\nprint('Prototype projected fitness:',72.124e6 * 10/4059766)\nprint('Gain over rounded champion:',(72.124e6 * 10/4059766 /139.32 - 1)*100)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
