nthe old entry on simultaneous lookup/training; no EX-to-IF table bypass\nis necessary because the prediction actually used travels with the fetch.\n\nCarry the selected predicted next PC in if_id_t and id_ex_t. When a fetch\nis actually accepted into ID/EX, advance the PC to that prediction; hold\nthe PC and prediction payload appropriately during load-use and data-bus\nstalls. Prediction itself must neither suppress the fetched instruction\nnor flush ID. In EX compute the architecturally resolved next PC using\nthe existing forwarded operands and misalignment/trap behavior. Compare\nit with the saved predicted next PC for EVERY valid instruction, including\nordinary i\n\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260930-003-r2s2\",\n  \"title\": \"64-entry next-PC prediction on the registered-divider champion\",\n \n…[21909 of 24981 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8cd0115617a137de]…\nation, and single-channel retirement interface.\n\nClassify legal aligned JAL/JALR instructions using the standard RISC-V\nlink-register hints: x1 and x5 are link registers. JAL pushes PC+4 when\nrd is a link register. For JALR, a link rs1 with a non-link rd pops;\na link rd with a non-link rs1 pushes; two identical link registers push;\ntwo different link registers pop then push. Neither link register means\nno stack action. A JALR requiring a pop is a return-prediction candidate\nonly when its immediate is zero; other JALRs keep their cached target\nprediction while performing the hinted stack action. Reserved encodings,\nillegal or misaligned transfers, bubbles and squashed instructions never\nchange the stack. Send classification and the real PC+4 push value with\nthe existing once-per-advance EX training event; do not add an EX-to-fetch\ndata bypass or move any existing predictor training to a later stage.\n\nMaintain a circular eight-word full-width stack with a next-write pointer\nand a saturating depth in 0..8. A p\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
