ls while leaving\nEX connected to its original raw training wires. Capture every payload\nfield unconditionally each clock; do not make payload enables depend on\ntrain_valid, branch outcome, redirect, or bus/arithmetic hold. Reset only\npending train_valid to zero. On other cycles capture the raw train_valid\nevery clock, including zero on a bubble or stalled EX instruction. This\nis a one-event-per-cycle pipeline register, not a holding queue: it must\naccept back-to-back training events, forward each exactly once in order,\nand never replay a pending event while EX is blocked. The raw valid \n…[22683 of 25755 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6d4042fa43b08713]…\nchanged.\n\nA four-bank storage prototype preserved all 4,059,766 CoreMark cycles\nand passed existing predictor/queue/pipeline tests and canonical CRC\nvalidation. It removed three predictor BSRAMs and shifted the worst\npath out of predictor maintenance, but its median 74.651 MHz regressed.\nA two-bank prototype retained the long EX-to-IF feedback path and\nregressed to median 70.932 MHz. The eight-bank option-0 probe measured\n78.372 MHz versus the parent's 76.000 MHz, using 6,359 logic cells,\n1,793 registers, 216 LUT-RAM units and four BSRAMs. Its worst path was\nin the divider, with fetch lookup next, rather than predictor training.\nChoose eight banks to bound local row decoding and update fanout more\ntightly. Forecast six percent fitness gain with low confidence;\noption-0's 3.1 percent gain alone is below the acceptance threshold,\nand the final median and lookup-mux routing remain unverified.\nAt unchanged cycles, acceptance requires median\nFmax above approximately 81.162 MHz and fitness above 199.922 iter/sec.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
