on actually used travels with the fetch.\n\nCarry the selected predicted next PC in if_id_t and id_ex_t. When a fetch\nis actually accepted into ID/EX, advance the PC to that prediction; hold\nthe PC and prediction payload appropriately during load-use and data-bus\nstalls. Prediction itself must neither suppress the fetched instruction\nnor flush ID. In EX compute the architecturally resolved next PC using\nthe existing forwarded operands and misalignment/trap behavior. Compare\nit with the saved predicted next PC for EVERY valid instruction, including\nordinary instructions and illegal encodings. Issue a corrective redirect\nand squash the younger fetch only when these next PCs differ\n…[16202 of 19274 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c9b73b65d15ad9be]…\ntrol fields, so validity-only\nrecovery cannot accidentally launch arithmetic or memory transactions.\nRetain all existing tests and unchanged decoder legality/trap checks.\nRun the existing pipeline/decoder/ALU suites, nret=1 formal gates, and\nstalled CoreMark cosim with canonical CRCs, UART and marker validation.\n\nJudge median Gowin Fmax times bracketed CoreMark iterations/cycle. The\nforecast is a 10% fitness gain: approximately 7% from the previously\nobserved cycle reduction, with modest extra timing margin from removing\nredirect-controlled instruction/payload masking on the current critical\npath. This timing gain is a hypothesis, not a measured result. With\n4,197,517 cycles, Fmax must exceed about 45.39 MHz to beat 103.38 by\n4.6%; if clock stays 46.433 MHz, cycles must be below about 4.294 million.\nTable routing, comparison/recovery fanout, and correctness of validity-\nonly bubbles are the main risks. Preserve the champion arithmetic and\nevaluate vendor timing; an IPC gain alone is insufficient acceptance.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
