{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n x=json.loads(line)\n if x.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(x['id'], x.get('title'), x.get('outcome'), 'fitness',x.get('fitness'),'fmax',x.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',x.get('cycles'))\n  h=x.get('hypothesis',{})\n  if isinstance(h,dict):\n   print('MOTIVATION:',h.get('motivation',''))\n  if x['id']=='hyp-20260930-003-r2s2':\n   print('ERROR:',x.get('error'),'NOTES:',x.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20260930-003-r1s2 Small tagged next-PC predictor removes taken-control bubbles regression fitness 12.81 fmax 5.393 cycles 4208674\nMOTIVATION: The baseline has no branch prediction: IF always selects PC+4, and EX\nredirects and squashes the combinational IF/ID instruction on every taken\nbranch, JAL, or JALR. Because IF/ID is not registered, this wastes one\nfetch opportunity per taken control transfer. CoreMark repeatedly executes\nloops and calls, so learning their direction and target should recover\nenough cycles to exceed the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold. The reported\nFmax bottleneck is the combinational arithmetic path; a small predictor\nshould improve iterations per cycle without lengthening that path. There\nare no recorded predictor attempts or distilled lessons in this fixture.\n\nhyp-20260930-003-r2s2 64-entry next-PC prediction on the registered-divider champion regression fitness 92.05 fmax 38.64 cycles 4197517\nMOTIVATION: The current champion already removes combinational division from the\ntimed execute path and reaches 38.602 MHz at fitness 85.94, but still\nfetches PC+4 and discards one younger fetch on every taken control\ntransfer. The earlier predictor experiment hyp-20260930-003-r1s2\nreduced stalled CoreMark cycles from 4,491,485 to 4,208,674 (6.3%) and\npassed formal and cosim. Its fitness remained 12.81 because it was\nbuilt on the old combinational-divider baseline, not the champion.\nThis is evidence for a useful predictor rather than evidence that\nprediction cannot help the current core. Recovering similar cycles\nat the champion's clock would yield about 6.7% more fitness, exceeding\nthe 4.6% acceptance threshold if predictor overhead stays small.\n\nERROR: None NOTES: \nhyp-20260930-003-r3s2 64-entry next-PC predictor with validity-only recovery improvement fitness 117.75 fmax 49.426 cycles 4197517\nMOTIVATION: The current hyp-20260930-002-r2s1 champion reaches fitness 103.38 at\n46.433 MHz but still loses a fetch opportunity on every taken branch,\nJAL, and JALR. The predictor attempts in log.jsonl \n…[1413 of 4485 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:341205f71f806c38]…\ntead, reduce the execution-stage cost of\nverifying and recovering predictions by computing operand-independent\nnext-PC candidates and their prediction equalities before ID/EX.\n\nAn isolated RTL prototype of this exact change, including both sequential\nand direct candidate registers, measured Gowin Fmax values of 50.255,\n52.536, and 52.536 MHz across placement options 0, 1, and 2. Its median\nis 52.536 MHz versus the champion's 49.426 MHz. It preserved exactly\n4,197,517 stalled CoreMark cycles, passed canonical UART/CRC validation\nwithout out-of-bounds accesses, and passed full reference trace cosim\nfor selftest and all three random programs with both bus stalls enabled.\nThese measurements imply approximately 125.16 iter/sec, a 6.3 percent\ngain over 117.75. The prototype has 3,584 logic cells and 966 registers,\nversus 3,662 and 902 for the champion. Formal remains to be run by the\nimplementation/evaluation phase; prototype measurements are supporting\nevidence and do not replace any correctness or fitness gate.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
