{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    e=json.loads(line)\n    if e.get('category')=='predictor':\n        print(e['id'], e['title'], 'outcome=',e['outcome'],'fitness=',e['fitness'],'cycles=',e.get('cycles'),'Fmax=',e.get('fmax_mhz'))\n        h=e['hypothesis']\n        print(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n        print('notes:',e.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20260930-003-r1s2 Small tagged next-PC predictor removes taken-control bubbles outcome= regression fitness= 12.81 cycles= 4208674 Fmax= 5.393\nAdd one 32-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged next-PC predictor inside IF.\nIndex with PC[6:2], tag with PC[31:7], and store an aligned target[31:2],\na conditional/unconditional bit, a two-bit saturating direction counter,\nand valid. Read asynchronously in parallel with instruction fetch, without\nadding a pipeline stage. On a valid tag hit, predict the cached target for\nan unconditional transfer or a conditional branch whose counter MSB is 1;\notherwise predict PC+4. This predicts JAL/JALR as well as conditional\nbranches through the same table; do not add another predictor mechanism.\nKeep the implementation small and reset only entry-valid bits, allowing\ntag/target/counter storage to infer compact memory where supported. Read\nthe old entry on simultaneous lookup/training; no EX-to-IF table bypass\nis necessary because the prediction actually used travels with the fetch.\n\nCarry the selected predicted next PC in if_id_t and id_ex_t. When a fetch\nis actually accepted into ID/EX, advance the PC to that prediction; hold\nthe PC and prediction payload appropriately during load-use and data-bus\nstalls. Prediction itself must neither suppress the fetched instruction\nnor flush ID. In EX compute the architecturally resolved next PC using\nthe existing forwarded operands and misalignment/trap behavior. Compare\nit with the saved predicted next PC for EVERY valid instruction, including\nordinary instructions and illegal encodings. Issue a corrective redirect\nand squash the younger fetch only when these next PCs differ. Correctly\npredicted taken transfers therefore capture their target instruction\ninstead of inserting the baseline bubble. A wrong taken prediction must\nrecover to PC+4; a wrong target must recover to the resolved target.\nRVFI pc_wdata must always report the resolved next PC, never a prediction.\n\nTrain exactly once when a valid EX instruction advances into EX/MEM, not\nwhile EX\n…[24357 of 27429 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:82372ed9b90f1072]…\ntrap rules, exact RVFI PCs/order, and the top-level IO contract.\n\nExtend pipeline tests for empty fall-through, filling while ID is held,\nfull-queue backpressure, simultaneous full enqueue/dequeue, head delivery\nwhile raw imemReady is low, and corrections at every occupancy. Exercise\nload-to-branch hazards using a head different from raw fetch data, older\nblocked loads/stores, MUL/DIV drain and response edges, stale entries,\nchanging JALR targets, illegal/misaligned transfers, reset, and wrong-path\nstores in both queue slots. Check exact retirement sequences and bounded\nprogress, then run unchanged formal gates and canonical stalled CoreMark\nwith CRC/UART, bounds, and timing-marker checks. Evaluate median Gowin\nFmax times measured bracketed iterations per cycle. Forecast an 8 percent\nfitness gain from hiding instruction stalls, with low confidence until\nmeasured. Queue mux/clock-enable routing and additional wrong-path fetches\nare the main timing and throughput risks; correctness checks stay intact.\n\nnotes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
