{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r7s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Deliver prediction recovery from the registered EX/MEM next PC'\nd['motivation']='''The champion already has a 64-entry fully tagged next-PC predictor and\ntwo-entry lookahead, with little measured remaining accuracy headroom.\nCurrent worst paths cross forwarding, EX, and IF. An isolated recovery\npipeline prototype measured median Gowin Fmax 59.444 MHz versus 56.560,\npassed all eight small-program reference checks, and completed canonical\nstalled CoreMark in 4,121,400 bracketed cycles versus 4,059,766. Its added\nrecovery cycle limited fitness improvement to roughly 3.5 percent. The\nexisting EX/MEM payload already registers the independently resolved\narchitectural next PC, suggesting a smaller recovery implementation that\ncan improve the clock margin without another full-width target register.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Deliver corrective fetch PCs through the existing EX/MEM architectural\npc_next register, with a one-cycle recovery-valid event and immediate\nsquashing of younger instructions. This is one predictor-recovery timing\nexperiment. Preserve the 64-entry predictor and its learning policy,\ntraining timing, two-entry fall-through queue, arithmetic, stage depth,\nand nret=1 external interface.\n\nEX continues to compare every advancing instruction's exact saved\nfull-width prediction with its independently resolved architectural next\nPC, including ordinary, illegal, misaligned, MUL, and DIV instructions.\nKeep the existing raw redirect and resolved target calculation. Register\nonly raw redirect into recovery_pending_q every clock, resetting this\nvalidity bit to zero. Never hold or replay the event because dmem or\narithmetic becomes blocked in the following cycle.\n\nWire IF's redirect input to recovery_pending_q and its redirect_target\nto ex_mem_w.pc_next. The instruction that generated raw redirect advanced\ninto EX/MEM on the detecting edge, so this registered architectural PC\nbelongs to that exact correction during the following cycle. The existing\nMUL/DIV saved-payload response transfer must carry the same resolved\nsequential PC for stale predictions; preserve those payloads and handshakes.\nDo not add another full-width target register or replace this PC with a\nprediction. Share the actual existing register rather than synthesizing\na duplicate target register with different enables. The pending event\nselects this registered target on the following edge even if that older\ninstruction now blocks in MEM.\n\nDecode must flush ID/EX.valid on BOTH raw EX redirect and pending recovery.\nThe raw event immediately kills the younger instruction on the detection\nedge. The pending event kills any speculative queue head/fall-through on\nthe following recovery edge, producing one extra bubble per mismatch.\nLeave raw instruction bits, register-file addresses, and ID/EX payload\ncapture enables independent of recovery. Invalid raw payloads must never\nlaunch arithmetic, memory, retirement, or further recovery.\n\nIF receives only the registered event. Apply it with priority over queue\nfullness, decode/data-memory/arithmetic holds, and raw imem backpressure;\nclear queue occupancy/pointers and load the saved actual target. Queue\nwrites or speculative fetch advances on the detecting edge may occur,\nbut all such younger work is discarded on registered recovery before it\ncan execute. Preserve immutable saved predictions and old-data lookup.\nKeep predictor training immediate and exactly once on the original\nqualified EX advance; the correcting instruction's own training remains\nvalid. No pending event can be cancelled by a later stall or redirect.\nReset cancels the pending event and resets IF/EX/MEM normally.\n\nRetain architectural resolved RVFI PCs, link values, trap/alignment checks,\nall register and memory side effects of older instructions, full-width\ntargets/tags, contiguous channel-0 retirement order, decoder defaults,\nmemory bounds, and real/ALTOPS arithmetic. Do not change any correctness\nor evaluation gate. Check that a correction from a stale prediction on\na load/store is delivered even when that same instruction subsequently\nwaits in MEM, without suppressing or duplicating its memory transaction.\n\nUpdate the independent queue model to use IF's delivered recovery event,\nwhile separately verifying the raw EX mismatch and immediate decode\nsquash. Add a recovery-event model proving one-cycle correspondence with\nthe generating instruction's architectural next PC, cancellation on reset,\nand invalid younger work on both edges. Cover all queue occupancies,\nboth branch directions, changing JALR targets, stale ordinary/illegal/\nMUL/DIV predictions, aligned/misaligned transfers, load-to-branch/JALR\nforwarding, older memory backpressure, and independent imem stalls.\nRetain every existing retirement, trap, queue, training, and wrong-path\nside-effect check. Run unchanged nret=1 formal and reference cosim gates,\nand canonical CRC/UART/bounds/marker-validated stalled CoreMark.\n\nCorrect predictions incur no extra cycle. The measured separate-target\nprototype's cycle increase was 1.52 percent; this register-sharing form\nshould retain that behavior, but measure its own bracketed count. At\n4,121,400 cycles, median Fmax must exceed about 60.064 MHz to beat fitness\n139.32 by 4.6 percent. Forecast five percent fitness improvement with low\nconfidence: reducing duplicated target storage and recovery routing must\nyield enough additional clock gain. The unchanged ALU/divider and training\npaths can limit the gain, and median vendor timing remains decisive.\n'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':5,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes']=[\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv'