{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_33", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('id')=='hyp-20260930-001-r4s0':\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Replace the three real combinational multiply expressions with ONE\nshared signed 33x33 multiplication, using operation-dependent sign\nextension on its inputs. Keep every multiplication at the existing\nsingle-cycle EX latency. This is one local arithmetic-sharing micro-\noptimization: retain the champion pipeline, predictor, divider,\nforwarding, bus stalls, retirement width, and architectural results.\n\nDefine a_signed = (op == ALU_MULH || op == ALU_MULHSU) and\nb_signed = (op == ALU_MULH). Form signed 33-bit operands\nmul_a = $signed({a_signed && a[31], a}) and\nmul_b = $signed({b_signed && b[31], b}). Compute an explicitly signed\n66-bit product = mul_a * mul_b. MUL returns product[31:0]; MULH,\nMULHU, and MULHSU all return product[63:32]. A zero leading bit\nrepresents every unsigned 32-bit source as positive; the leading\noperand sign bit represents signed sources exactly. The 66-bit\nintermediate also represents unsigned products above 2^63 without\ntreating them as negative. Do not use 32-bit signed casts, discard\ncarry into bit 32, or apply serial post-multiply sign corrections.\nMUL deliberately uses unsigned inputs since its low 32 bits are\nidentical for all signedness interpretations.\n\nPut this single multiplication in the real arithmetic ifdef branch,\nwith no independently connected mul_ss, mul_uu, or mul_su expressions.\nKeep the four exact documented RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas in their\nexisting branch. Preserve every nonmultiply ALU result and the entire\nregistered divider transaction, including its launch seed, seven\niterations, registered completion, and held-response handshake.\nUse portable RTL so Gowin can infer its native large multiplier;\nno vendor primitives, extra arithmetic stages, or clock changes.\nMake all three high-half multiply cases select the same product\nslice, so signedness selection is confined to two input extension\nbits rather than three wide result networks. The intended benefit\nis fewer DSP blocks, lower operand fanout, and simpler high-product\nselection and routing on the measured critica\n…[1169 of 4241 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3841f540eb252b2e]…\ns deterministic random pairs. Compare\nall four operations against Python integer products. Exercise\nalternating multiply variants with forwarded ALU/load sources,\ndependent ALU/store/branch consumers, x0 destinations, and imem/dmem\nstalls. Verify exact RVFI source values and results, contiguous\norder, and exactly-once memory and retirement effects. Keep decoder\nillegal defaults, traps, reset, bus handshakes, and all hard checks.\nRun unchanged nret=1 formal gates and real-arithmetic ISS/CoreMark\ncosim with canonical CRCs, required UART text, and MMIO timing\nbrackets. ALTOPS formal alone does not prove real multiplication.\n\nForecast an 8 percent fitness improvement from Fmax at unchanged\nCoreMark cycles, with medium confidence. Median Fmax must exceed\napproximately 51.70 MHz at the current cycle count to beat the\n117.75 champion by more than 4.6 percent. Placement-option-0 gains\nalone do not establish this; operation-dependent sign-bit routing\nand the now-near-critical divider launch are the main limitations.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
